## SURF Willma gpt-oss-120b

When using model `surf-ai-hub/openai/gpt-oss-120b` (roles: surf, eb-stack, surf-plan):

- Prefer the **apply_patch** tool for file edits (Codex-style *** Begin Patch).
- Do not invent commercial models; SURF/eb-stack work stays on Willma only.

### eb-stack driver contract

- Print-mode driver invocations MUST pin the toolbox:
  `omp -p --model surf-ai-hub/openai/gpt-oss-120b --tools=bash,read,edit,apply_patch,resolve,search_tool_bm25 ...`
  (the task/delegation tool loses bounded work in -p; validated 2026-07-10:
  three runs flaky with it available, deterministic pass without it).
  `resolve` + `search_tool_bm25` keep MCP tools reachable: the eb-stack MCP
  server (registered in `~/.omp/agent/mcp.json`) serves `eb_check_recipe` /
  `eb_bump` / `eb_solve` with the reporting ladder and next-actions embedded
  in every result — prefer those typed tools over shelling out to the CLI.

- **Execute directly with your own tools** (shell + apply_patch). Do NOT
  delegate to sub-agents/task tools for bounded work — a check-recipe run,
  a recipe edit, and a re-check is three commands you run yourself; a
  handoff that produces no edit is a silent failure (observed 2026-07-10:
  a lint-fix task ended at "delegating to a sub-agent" with the file
  untouched).
- Finish the loop before reporting: if the task says fix-and-re-verify,
  the report must contain the re-run's actual output, not the plan.
- Read BOTH documents before acting: the skill
  `~/Git/Github/Tools/eb-stack/skills/annual-bump/SKILL.md` (portable
  procedure) and the site runbook
  `~/Git/Gitlab/obsidian-notes/Software/SURF/eb_stack_site_runbook.org`
  (exact per-machine incantations: rg.terra, elja, Snellius). The skill's
  section 10 rules are the execution contract, not suggestions.
- Run the REAL `eb-stack` CLI (`check-recipe`, `bump`, `solve`); never guess
  dependency versions or reimplement EasyBuild semantics in prose.
- Read tool output as instructions: a missing-dep hint ("available at other
  generations: ...") is your work queue; a `[packaging]` checksum finding
  means reorder the recipe's checksums (sources first, then patches) — never
  bypass or delete the check.
- Report with the three-claim ladder every time: resolves (check-recipe
  exit 0) / builds (eb --robot green) / binary-verified (`env -i <bin>
  --version` + ldd). Never claim a higher rung than you executed.
- NEVER compile or run heavy builds on this laptop — remote builders only
  (per the site runbook). Running an already-built binary locally is fine.
- NEVER touch the GitHub/GitLab PR or issue surface (no gh/glab/eb --new-pr,
  no comments): branch pushes to HaoZeke/* forks are allowed plumbing;
  everything PR-facing goes to a paste-ready draft under
  `~/Git/Gitlab/obsidian-notes/Software/IssuePRs/<repo>/` for the human.
- One PR per recipe set exists at a time; if asked to prepare PR text,
  check for existing open PRs for the same software first and say so.
